// PiHub / Home Drive: shared tailnet ACL policy (template).
//
// This is NOT applied automatically. Tailscale ACLs live in the Tailscale
// admin console (or are pushed via its API), not in this repo. Paste this
// into https://login.tailscale.com/admin/acls (or your coordination
// server's equivalent), replace every REPLACE-ME placeholder with your own
// values, and save.
//
// ── What this policy actually does ──────────────────────────────────────
// Tailscale's own default policy is permissive: every device in the
// tailnet can reach every other device, on every port, once it's an
// accepted member. That default is almost certainly wider than you want;
// see ../docs/DEVICE-ONBOARDING.md for why. This policy narrows it to:
//   1. Only devices tagged tag:approved-device can reach anything at all.
//   2. Even those can only reach each server on the specific port(s) its
//      own `tailscale serve` actually answers on. See the per-service
//      rules below. Every other port a service happens to have open
//      (Navidrome's 4533, Jellyfin's 8096, PiHub's raw nginx :80, etc.)
//      stays unreachable over the tailnet even though the node itself is a
//      tailnet member. Those are LAN-only by design; joining a tailnet
//      does not change that unless you let it, and this policy doesn't.
//   3. tagOwners below controls who is allowed to actually apply these
//      tags to a device in the first place. Replace the placeholder
//      email with your own Tailscale login. Without this block, nobody
//      (including you) can tag a device at all.
//
// ── One tag per SERVER, not one tag for everything ──────────────────────
// Each Pi/stack advertises its own tag (tag:home-drive-server,
// tag:pihub-server) rather than a single shared tag:home-server. Two
// separate ACL rules below, one per service, each scoped to exactly the
// port that service's own `tailscale serve` answers on:
//   - home-drive: 443 only. home-drive is reachable directly on the LAN by
//     default; this tag only matters if you also enable its optional
//     `tailscale` compose profile, to reach it from outside the LAN.
//   - pihub:      443 only. PiHub is a single stack covering all media
//     streaming (music, movies, shows, photos), the home dashboard, and
//     everything formerly reachable as a standalone PiTune. One tag and
//     one port cover all of it, because PiHub's own nginx is the single
//     thing `tailscale serve` proxies to; there is nothing behind it that
//     needs its own tag.
// This also means a device only needs to be granted the SPECIFIC service
// rules it should reach. See "Scoping a device to one service" below.
//
// ── The "only devices I approve" requirement, and its real mechanism ────
// Tags are not something a device can grant itself. Only whichever
// login(s) you list under tagOwners can apply tag:approved-device to a
// device (via `tailscale up --advertise-tags=tag:approved-device`, or from
// the admin console's device list). Combine this with tailnet-wide
// "device approval" (Settings → Device management → require approval for
// new devices) and you get the actual, enforceable version of "I decide
// which devices get in": a new device authenticating to your tailnet sits
// pending until you approve it AND it has no access to anything until you
// (specifically, not the device itself) tag it tag:approved-device. See
// ../docs/DEVICE-ONBOARDING.md for the full setup, including the
// recommended "enroll it while physically on your home Wi-Fi, approve and
// tag it, then it also works from anywhere" workflow. Tailscale itself
// has no concept of "was on the LAN during setup," so this workflow is
// what actually delivers that property in practice.
//
// ── Scoping a device to one service only (e.g. a guest) ─────────────────
// tag:approved-device below is granted both services by default, which is
// the common case for your own personal devices. To hand a guest access to
// just PiHub's music and nothing else: add a second device tag (e.g.
// tag:approved-device-pihub) to tagOwners, give it its own single ACL rule
// against tag:pihub-server only, and tag their device with that instead of
// tag:approved-device. Never add tag:approved-device itself to a device
// you don't want full access for. It is intentionally "both, full stop".
//
// ── "Only 2 devices at once" ─────────────────────────────────────────────
// Deliberately NOT implemented as a live concurrent-connection counter.
// See ../README.md's "Why not a literal concurrent-connection cap" section
// for the reasoning. The way this policy actually bounds it: tag exactly
// as many devices tag:approved-device as you want allowed, ever. Tag two
// devices and *only* those two can reach anything, full stop, not "the
// first two to connect at a given moment." Untag a device (or remove it
// from the tailnet) to revoke it; tag a different one to swap it in.
{
	"tagOwners": {
		"tag:home-drive-server": ["REPLACE-ME-your-login@example.com"],
		"tag:pihub-server":      ["REPLACE-ME-your-login@example.com"],
		"tag:approved-device":   ["REPLACE-ME-your-login@example.com"],
	},

	"acls": [
		{
			"action": "accept",
			"src":    ["tag:approved-device"],
			"dst":    ["tag:home-drive-server:443"],
		},
		{
			"action": "accept",
			"src":    ["tag:approved-device"],
			"dst":    ["tag:pihub-server:443"],
		},
	],

	// Belt-and-suspenders default-deny reminder: any src/dst pair not
	// listed in "acls" above is denied automatically. Tailscale ACLs are
	// allowlists; there is no separate "deny" rule needed. If a device you
	// expect to work can't reach a server, the fix is almost always "it
	// isn't tagged tag:approved-device yet, or that Pi isn't advertising
	// its own tag:*-server tag yet," not a missing deny rule.

	// Optional but recommended: require the tags above to be applied
	// before a node can do anything at all, closing the gap where a freshly
	// approved but not-yet-tagged device would otherwise inherit whatever
	// your tailnet's default (no rule matches -> deny) already handles.
	// This "grants" block is intentionally left out because the deny-by-
	// default behavior above already covers it; don't add a blanket
	// "tag:*-server" -> "tag:approved-device" rule unless you have a
	// concrete reason a server needs to reach back to a client, which none
	// of these stacks do.
}
